Ticket fields
-------------

*Status: draft design, not implemented yet.*

Currently, the fields of a ticket are hard-coded: an open/closed status, the
seven severities of the Debian BTS, tags, an owner, a milestone, a deadline and
a progress percentage. To make LightBTS usable for tracking issues in general,
the fields should be defined in the configuration of each instance instead.

## Goals

- Each instance can define its own fields, the values they can have, and which
  statuses and ticket types exist.
- Messages stay the only source of truth. Fields are changed by pseudo-headers,
  as they are now, and the index is derived from the messages.
- Existing instances keep working without changes: the default definitions
  give exactly the current behavior.
- The command line and web interfaces work with any field, instead of having
  code for each one.

## Defining fields

Fields are defined in sections of `.lightbts/config` named `field.<name>`:

    [field.priority]
        type = enum
        values = low, normal, high, urgent
        default = normal
        ordered = true

    [field.component]
        type = set
        values = core, cli, web, email

    [field.estimate]
        type = number
        min = 0
        unit = h

The variables of a field are:

`type`:
: The type of the field, see below. Required.
`values`:
: For `enum` and `set` fields, the allowed values, separated by commas.
For `set` fields, this is optional; without it, any value is allowed.
`default`:
: The value of the field when it is not set.
`ordered`:
: For `enum` fields, whether the values are in a meaningful order, so that
tickets can be sorted by the field, and filtered with comparisons such as
`priority>=high`.
`min`, `max`, `unit`:
: For `number` fields.
`aliases`:
: Other names for the pseudo-header, separated by commas, for example
`topic` for `title`.
`label`:
: The name shown in the interfaces, if it should differ from the name.

Values of `enum` and `set` fields are single words (letters, digits, `-` and
`_`), so they can be used in pseudo-headers and on the command line without
quoting. They are shown with `-` and `_` replaced by spaces, and the first
letter capitalized.

### Types

`text`:
: A single line of text. For example: a milestone.
`enum`:
: One value out of a fixed list. For example: severity, priority, ticket type.
`set`:
: Any number of values, optionally out of a fixed list. For example: tags,
components.
`user`:
: An email address, possibly with a name. For example: the owner of a ticket.
Once LightBTS has identities (see `ROADMAP.md`), these can be checked and
shown with the person's name.
`date`:
: A date in the form `YYYY-MM-DD`. For example: a deadline.
`number`:
: An integer. For example: progress, or an estimate.

### The status field

The `status` field is required, and must be an `enum`. Each status belongs to
one of three categories:

`to-do`:
: Work that has not started yet.
`in-progress`:
: Work that has started.
`done`:
: Work that is finished, for whatever reason: fixed, rejected, a duplicate.

Tickets in the `to-do` and `in-progress` categories are open, and tickets in
the `done` category are closed. LightBTS needs to know the categories so that
`lbts list` can show open tickets by default, and so that later, boards and
reports can tell work that has not started from work in progress. It also needs
to know what `lbts close` and `lbts reopen` should do:

    [field.status]
        type = enum
        values = new, confirmed, in-progress, fixed, wontfix
        default = new
        in-progress = in-progress
        done = fixed, wontfix
        close = fixed
        reopen = confirmed

`in-progress`, `done`:
: The values that belong to these categories. All other values belong to the
`to-do` category.
`close`, `reopen`:
: The values that `lbts close` and `lbts reopen` set. The value of `close` must
be in the `done` category, and the values of `reopen` and `default` must not.

The categories are not stored in the index, since they follow from the status
and the field definitions.

The selectors `open` and `closed` of `lbts list` select tickets by whether
their status is open or closed, not by a status named "open" or "closed".
Likewise, the selectors `to-do`, `in-progress` and `done` select tickets by the
category of their status. To select a status with the same name as a category,
use `status=in-progress`.

Rules for which transitions between statuses are allowed, and who may make
them, are part of the workflows described in `ROADMAP.md`, and are not part of
this design. The configuration leaves room for them in the same section.

### Ticket types

Ticket types are just an `enum` field named `type`, which instances can choose
to define:

    [field.type]
        type = enum
        values = bug, feature, task
        default = bug

Later, other fields could be limited to certain ticket types, for example with
`types = bug` in the section of the `severity` field.

### Default fields

`lbts init` writes the following definitions into the configuration of a new
instance, so they are easy to find and change:

    [field.status]
        type = enum
        values = open, closed
        default = open
        done = closed
        close = closed
        reopen = open

    [field.severity]
        type = enum
        values = wishlist, minor, normal, important, serious, critical, grave
        default = normal
        ordered = true

    [field.tags]
        type = set
        aliases = tag

    [field.owner]
        type = user

    [field.milestone]
        type = text

    [field.deadline]
        type = date

    [field.progress]
        type = number
        min = 0
        max = 100
        unit = %

If the configuration of an instance has no `field.*` sections at all, as is
the case for existing instances, these definitions are used. Otherwise, the
configuration is complete by itself: a field that is not defined doesn't
exist, so for example an instance can do without severities.

### Built-in pseudo-headers

Some pseudo-headers are not fields, and their names can't be used for fields:

- `Title` and `Topic`, since the title is always there, and initially comes
  from the Subject header.
- `Version`, `Found`, `Notfound`, `Fixed` and `Notfixed`, which record in which
  versions a problem was found or fixed. Their meaning depends on the status of
  the ticket, so they don't fit the field types.
- The link types, like `Blocks` and `Unblocks`. Making these configurable,
  with their reverse names, is left for later.

## Changing fields

A pseudo-header whose name is the name or an alias of a field changes that
field. Names are case-insensitive.

For fields that have a single value:

    Priority: high
    Priority: -

The value `-` unsets the field, after which it has its default value, if any.
This is what `Owner: -` already does.

For `set` fields, the syntax of the `Tags` pseudo-header is used: `+value` or
`value` adds, `-value` removes, and `=value` replaces all values. A `+`, `-` or
`=` on its own applies to the following values:

    Component: web -cli
    Component: = core email

A value that is not valid for the field, like `Priority: highest`, is ignored
and reported as a warning, as invalid values are now. The message is still
added to the ticket.

The headers `X-LightBTS-Status` and `X-LightBTS-Tag` become a general rule: an
email header `X-LightBTS-<field>` is treated as a pseudo-header for that field.

## The index

Field values are stored in a single table, replacing the `tags` table and the
`status`, `severity`, `owner`, `deadline`, `progress` and `milestone` columns
of the `bugs` table:

    CREATE TABLE fields (bug INTEGER, name TEXT, value TEXT,
                         PRIMARY KEY(bug, name, value),
                         FOREIGN KEY(bug) REFERENCES bugs(id))
    CREATE INDEX fields_name_value_index ON fields (name, value)

A field with a single value has one row, a `set` field has one row per value,
and an unset field has no rows. Values are stored as text, also for `enum`
fields, so that reordering the values in the configuration doesn't change the
meaning of the index. The `bugs` table keeps the columns that are not fields:
`id`, `title`, `submitter` and `date`.

Since the index is built from the messages, its schema can change freely:

- The index version becomes 5. When LightBTS opens an index with version 4,
  it rebuilds the index automatically, as `lbts reindex` does.
- Which values are valid depends on the field definitions, so the index
  depends on them as well. The index stores a hash of the field definitions.
  When they have changed, the command line interface rebuilds the index
  automatically before doing anything else, and says so. The web interface,
  which only reads the index, logs a warning instead.
- `lbts fsck` reports when the index was built with other field definitions.

## Command line interface

- `lbts set <id> <field> <value>...` and `lbts unset <id> <field>` change any
  field. The existing commands `severity`, `owner`, `noowner`, `milestone`,
  `deadline`, `progress` and `tags` keep working, as shortcuts for these, as
  long as the corresponding field exists.
- `-F, --field <field>=<value>` sets a field when creating a ticket or
  replying, generalizing `-S` and `-T`, which stay as shortcuts.
- `lbts list` accepts selectors of the form `<field>=<value>`,
  `<field>!=<value>`, and for ordered `enum`, `number` and `date` fields also
  `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`. The existing selectors keep working: `open`,
  `closed` and `all`, the status categories, a value of the `severity` field,
  and otherwise a tag.
  Note that `<` and `>` need quoting in the shell.
- `lbts show` shows all fields that are set, in the order they are defined.
- `lbts fields` shows the field definitions, with their values.

## Web interface

- The filter form gets a selection for each `enum` field, and an input for
  each `set` field.
- Which fields are shown as columns in the ticket list is configured with
  `web.columns`, which defaults to `status, severity, title` if those fields
  exist.
- The ticket page shows all fields that are set.

## Implementation steps

1. Parse and validate the field definitions, with the built-in defaults.
   Split `lbts config` names at the last dot, so that `lbts config
   field.priority.type` works.
2. Add the `fields` table, the automatic rebuild of older indexes, and the hash
   of the field definitions. Apply pseudo-headers to fields.
3. Replace the uses of the old columns and the `tags` table in the core, the
   command line and the web interface.
4. Add `lbts set`, `lbts unset`, `--field`, the new `lbts list` selectors and
   `lbts fields`.
5. Make the web interface use the field definitions.

## Open questions

- Should a ticket's field values be shown with their history, for example in
  `lbts show --history`? The messages already contain the full history, so
  this needs no extra storage.
